DevOps Interview Preparation Series — DevOps Engineer vs Site Reliability Engineer (SRE) ⚙️ 

In today’s tech world, the roles of DevOps and SRE often overlap — both aim to bridge the gap between development and operations, but the approach, mindset, and daily responsibilities are very different.

If you’re preparing for a DevOps or SRE interview, or planning your career growth path — this post will help you clearly understand the real-world difference 👇

DevOps Engineer — The Builder & Automator
A DevOps Engineer focuses on automation, CI/CD, infrastructure as code, and deployment pipelines.
Their primary goal is to accelerate software delivery while maintaining stability and scalability.

Core Responsibilities:
Automate build, test, and deployment processes.
Design CI/CD pipelines (Jenkins, GitHub Actions, GitLab CI).
Manage Infrastructure as Code (Terraform, CloudFormation, Ansible).
Work on containerization and orchestration (Docker, Kubernetes).
Set up monitoring & alerting systems for deployments.
Collaborate with developers for faster and reliable releases.
Implement DevSecOps practices to integrate security in pipelines.

Mindset:
“How can we automate everything and deploy faster with confidence?”
Example:
A DevOps Engineer ensures code is tested, built, containerized, and deployed automatically — say, via Jenkins → Docker → EKS — with zero manual steps.


Site Reliability Engineer (SRE) — The Guardian of Reliability
A Site Reliability Engineer focuses on system reliability, scalability, performance, and incident response.
 Their job is to maintain service uptime, ensure observability, and manage operational excellence at scale.

Core Responsibilities:
Maintain reliability of production systems and SLAs/SLOs.
Implement monitoring, alerting, and incident management (Prometheus, Grafana, Datadog).
Conduct root cause analysis and post-incident reviews.
Develop automation to reduce toil and manual ops work.
Manage capacity planning, scaling, and failover strategies.
Enforce reliability metrics (SLOs, SLIs, error budgets).
Collaborate with developers to improve fault tolerance in applications.

Mindset:
“How can we make the system reliable, observable, and self-healing?”
Example:
An SRE ensures a microservice in production automatically restarts on failure, scales based on load, and alerts only when meaningful — maintaining 99.9% uptime.
